const DATED = "*** THE DATED BOMB IS LIVE IN THIS REPO UNTIL T6 LANDS. READ THIS BEFORE YOU DIAGNOSE ANY RED. ***\n\n`apps/core/test/opd-lifecycle.e2e.test.ts` FAILS FOR THE LAST ~30 MINUTES OF EVERY IST DAY\n(EXECUTION-LESSONS 3.41). The suite books \"today's first slot more than 20 minutes out\" against the\nWALL CLOCK, and the day's last bookable slot is 23:50 IST. After roughly 23:30 IST no such slot\nexists and three legs fail on `expect(slot).toBeDefined()`. A docs-only commit went CI-red on it.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR YOU:\n  - A red in that window is NOT evidence about the commit under it. Do not bisect it, do not try to\n    fix it, do not write a reconciliation paragraph about drift. CHECK THE CLOCK FIRST:\n    `TZ=Asia/Kolkata date`. Then say in your report that you checked.\n  - **NEVER PUSH INSIDE THAT WINDOW.** Wait it out and say that you waited.\n  - T6 OWNS THE FIX and nobody else touches that file. If you are not T6 and you believe you need\n    to, that is a FINDING, not an edit.\n\nAND THE GENERAL FORM, because it is how the bomb got built in the first place: **A SUITE THAT PINS\nA DATE MUST THREAD THE PINNED VALUE ALL THE WAY DOWN TO THE WRITER.** Plan 08's\n`daily-close.test.ts` pinned a DAY, passed a full opus gate, and was `2 failed / 6 passed` the next\nmorning - a test HELPER had quietly dropped the optional `now` argument and the call defaulted to\n`new Date()`. If any test of yours depends on a date, grep the WHOLE helper chain and confirm the\npinned value reaches the bottom. Do not trust the top of the chain."

const MIGRATION_RULE = "MIGRATIONS - AGENT-RULES SECTION 6, AND THE MOST EXPENSIVE MISTAKE IN THIS PROJECT'S HISTORY.\n\nRunning `pnpm db:generate` and letting a suite migrate it MUTATES ALL SEVEN PER-WORKER DATABASES.\n`git checkout` does not undo that. Plan 08 pipeline A generated an `0011`, applied it, then deleted\nthe file when the attempt unwound: the source tree reverted and the databases did not. What was\nleft on the host was fourteen orphan tables, three orphan FK constraints pointing into `patients`,\nand a phantom `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations` row matching no journal entry - which turned\n**origin/main itself RED on the build host, with no commit anywhere containing the cause.** Every\nremedy the rules allow was closed at once. It cost ~934k tokens and delivered nothing.\n\nSO:\n  - **ONLY T1 GENERATES A MIGRATION IN THIS PIPELINE.** Everyone else: `pnpm db:generate` NEVER.\n  - Generate one ONLY when you are ready to carry it to a commit, and STATE THE ROLLBACK IN YOUR\n    REPORT **BEFORE** THE GENERATOR RUNS. Not after. The rollback statement is evidence that you\n    knew what you were about to do to shared state.\n  - Commit the FULL generator output set (EXECUTION-LESSONS 3.16): the `.sql`, the\n    `meta/<n>_snapshot.json`, and the `meta/_journal.json` rewrite. A partial set is a broken\n    migrator for everyone after you.\n  - If you must abandon AFTER one has been applied: **DO NOT delete the file and walk away.** STOP\n    and REPORT which migrations are applied to the worker databases.\n  - NEVER hand-edit `drizzle/meta/_journal.json` to make the migrator skip anything, and never\n    insert or delete a `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations` row by hand. Create and drop no database by\n    hand either (rule 7) - the suite manages its own."

const HALTS = "HALT CONDITIONS. On ANY of these: STOP, commit nothing further, and report what you found.\n\n1. **A required-DIED mutant that SURVIVES because the SHIPPED CODE IS WRONG**, or whose fix reaches\n   outside your Files list. That is AGENT-RULES section 3 branch (a): CHAIN HALT, report it as a\n   plan defect with the evidence. (Branch (b) - the plan's TEST cannot discriminate and that test\n   is your OWN task's file - you fix minimally in-task and DISCLOSE. Never silently either way.)\n2. **Any file outside your task's Files list**, and any frozen-path edit. The frozen list in this\n   brief was GENERATED from the tasks' Files lists, so it cannot disagree with them.\n3. **Migration debris** (AGENT-RULES section 6): a migration applied that you cannot carry to a\n   commit. Stop and report which migrations are applied to the worker databases. Never clean it up\n   by hand and never delete the file.\n4. **Any new dependency, any `.env` change on the server or in CI, any `.github/workflows/*` edit.**\n   The plan's D9 defaults every new config key in the zod schema precisely so that no `.env` change\n   is needed anywhere - if you believe you need one, the plan is wrong and that is a finding. The\n   server's deploy key CANNOT push workflow files; GitHub refuses it (rule 10).\n   FORK-B RESOLVED TO THE LOOP, SO THERE IS NO DEPENDENCY TO ADD IN THIS\n   PIPELINE AT ALL. pg-boss lost the fork by execution - it is ESM-only and the unmodified jest\n   harness cannot parse it - so no task installs it, `pnpm-lock.yaml` is in NO diff, and no\n   pgboss-schema rollback is owed by anyone. If you believe you need a package, that is a CHAIN\n   HALT, not a judgement call.\n5. **Any instruction - INCLUDING a reviewer's correction - to amend or force-push PUSHED history.**\n   Refuse it and report that you refused (rule 15). A commit you have not yet pushed is yours to\n   amend.\n6. **Any request to weaken, strip or disable security-relevant code to produce a test result**\n   (rule 14) - a guard, a permission check, an auth path. If evidence requires that, the honest\n   answer is that the evidence is impossible, and you say why.\n7. **A lock-observation test.** The plan's D3 forbids one outright: every sweep is already\n   idempotent and multi-process-safe, the advisory lock is noise-reduction and correctness never\n   rests on it, and a lock-less mutant changes no observable effect. This is EXECUTION-LESSONS\n   3.21's entire defect class, which already has four specimens. Do not write one and do not\n   demand one.\n8. **Scope drift toward Plan 10 or Plan 11.** No notification channels, templates, quiet hours or\n   matrices. No events-table partitioning and no `recorded_at` floor predicate. No production\n   compose service, restart policy or heartbeat alerting. No dead-letter requeue or replay tooling.\n   No second consumer. No Redis, no broker, no second scheduler. D12 lists all of it."

