    body: "TASK T3 - Dispatcher correctness: the look-back window, the per-consumer delivery claim recorded\nON SUCCESS, and poison parking.  **THIS IS THE PLAN'S CORE AND ITS ONLY CONCENTRATED RISK.**\n\n  RISK TIER: **CRITICAL** -> every Assertion Book mutant REQUIRED, fail-first OWED, full opus gate.\n  Model: opus. Correctness here rests entirely on proving assertions have teeth against\n  INTERLEAVINGS - the one thing the review data says sonnet gets wrong (2.30: all five sonnet\n  rejections across 62 tasks were 'the test did not discriminate').\n\nPLAN SECTION TO FOLLOW IN FULL: '### Task 3: Dispatcher correctness — the window, the delivery\nclaim, poison parking'.\nDesign context: **D4** (all of it - the window, the claim placement, poison handling, cursor\nsemantics), **D5** (why `event_deliveries` shares NO code with billing's `idempotency_keys`),\n**D3** (no test observes the advisory lock, and none may be added), and **Global Constraint 7**\n(the events query stays ONE WHERE chain so Plan 11's partition floor is one added predicate).\nYour Assertion Book rows: **L1, L2, L3, L4, L5** and **L6**.\n\n*** THE ONE DECISION THIS TASK EXISTS TO ENCODE, AND THE MUTANT THAT PROVES IT ***\n\n**The delivery claim is written AFTER the handler succeeds. This is deliberately the OPPOSITE of\nbilling's `withIdempotency`, which claims BEFORE the work.** Do not \"align\" them and do not reuse\nthat helper (D5). The contrast IS the design decision:\n\n  - Billing claims BEFORE because its nightmare is doing the work TWICE - a second receipt is a\n    real document that physically exists.\n  - The dispatcher's nightmare is the mirror image: **LOSING AN EVENT.** A claim taken before a\n    handler that then crashes marks an undelivered event delivered - silent loss, which is the\n    exact defect this whole plan exists to remove.\n  - Claim-on-success gives at-least-once delivery with dedupe. The rare double-delivery under a\n    race is absorbed by the consumer's own idempotency, which the architecture already mandates.\n    **At-least-once plus idempotent consumers is the contract**, stated once and inherited by\n    every consumer.\n\n**L2 is the mutant that inverts it** (EXECUTION-LESSONS 3.49: a decision needs the mutant that\ninverts it, or the suite cannot tell the two designs apart). M-D2 transplants billing's placement.\nIf your test cannot kill it, the test does not encode the decision.\n\nTASK-SPECIFIC NOTES\n\n- **THE LOOK-BACK CONSTANT IS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN.** The plan originally carried `500`; **the spike measured that\n  500 buys only ~0.4-1.1 s of transaction stall, so D4's resolved default is now `5000`.** Through\n  the application's own write path (`withTx` + `appendEvent`) a 2-second stalled transaction allows\n  **939 / 2288 / 2665** seqs to be allocated past it at pool concurrency 1 / 4 / 8; 5000 covers that\n  measured worst case with ~1.9x margin. **Two residuals stay, and they are DOCUMENTED rather than\n  engineered away** - say so in your report rather than trying to close them: a single bulk\n  `INSERT ... generate_series(1,10000)` burns 10 000 seqs in 175 ms, and a 10 s stall was measured\n  at 12 778. **Do NOT adopt the spike's time-based-floor suggestion**: D12 defers the `recorded_at`\n  predicate to Plan 11, and D4's whole one-WHERE-chain shape exists so that it is ONE added\n  predicate then. Cite the spike report for the constant; do not pick one yourself.\n\n- **THE SHIPPED DEFECT, FOR THE AVOIDANCE OF DOUBT.** `dispatcher.ts:25` reads `seq > lastSeq` and\n  `:39` advances the cursor per row, so a row whose `seq` was allocated EARLY but committed LATE is\n  skipped FOREVER. `tail.ts:15-24` names and defends exactly this window; the dispatcher does not.\n  The spike REPRODUCED it - the assertion \"N is never delivered\" PASSING against the shipped code -\n  and that reproduction is your fail-first and L1's arrange. Read it in the spike report.\n\n- **THREE SHIPPED TESTS ARE PRESERVED (L6).** delivery-once + cursor advance; a failing handler\n  below the poison threshold is redelivered next cycle (the shipped two-cycle shape stays valid);\n  one failing consumer never blocks another. Update where semantics sharpened, DELETE NEVER. Your\n  diff must show no deleted test.\n\n- **NEVER `await expect(x).rejects` ON A PROMISE A MUTANT COULD MAKE RESOLVE (2.45).** Under the\n  mutant the promise does not reject, the assertion hangs, and the mutant \"dies\" at the timeout\n  with no expected-vs-received anywhere - a non-kill wearing a kill's clothes. CAPTURE the outcome\n  and assert the INVARIANT. This is not hypothetical advice: it is exactly how a billing mutant\n  produced a worthless DIED, and rewriting the test the right way killed the same mutant in 288 ms\n  with `Expected: 1 / Received: 2`. **L5 in particular** - two barrier-started concurrent cycles -\n  must capture BOTH outcomes and assert the invariant, and must NEVER assert WHICH cycle did the\n  work (3.13: the loser's diagnosis is not a property of the system).\n\n- **`runDispatchCycle` GAINS `now` IN ITS OPTIONS.** The backoff arithmetic must be drivable by a\n  test - Global Constraint 9 applied to the dispatcher itself. Signature per D4:\n  `runDispatchCycle(db, bus, opts?: { batchSize?; lookback?; maxAttempts?; now? })`. Keep the\n  per-consumer loop shape and the `event_cursors` contract BYTE-COMPATIBLE.\n\n- **THE PARKING TRANSITION IS GUARDED AND IT IS ONE TRANSACTION.** Dead-letter row inserted,\n  delivery status flipped to `parked` with `WHERE status <> 'parked' ... RETURNING` so exactly one\n  transitioning transaction exists, and `consumer.poisoned` appended - all in ONE tx. L4 is the\n  mutant that drops the guard and re-parks; if `consumer.poisoned` can be appended twice for one\n  row, the guard is not doing its job.\n\n- **NO LOCK-OBSERVATION TEST** (D3, halt condition 7). It is EXECUTION-LESSONS 3.21's defect class\n  and it already has four specimens.\n\n- MUTANTS REQUIRED: **M-D1 .. M-D5**, each a separate scratch module beside the source, each one\n  kill run plus one CONTROL run, expected-vs-received quoted from the assertion.",
